================================================================================
ATTRIBUTION
================================================================================

The MIT grant above is the licence and stands on its own. This section is
informational: it records what this repository implements, what it bundles,
and what it depends on.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. The work implemented
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Tomoki Mihara, "p-adic Character Neural Network",
arXiv:2603.29905v1 [math.NT], 31 March 2026.
https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.29905
Distributed on arXiv under the arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license.

This repository is an independent implementation, for p = 2, of the algorithms
described in that paper -- principally Algorithm 6 (digit dynamic programming
for the largest e admitting a common zero modulo p^e) and the Section 4
reduction from a p-adic character network to a polynomial system. No text,
figures, or code from the paper are reproduced here. Where the printed
pseudocode is transcribed, it is transcribed as an expression of the published
algorithm; three places where this implementation departs from the printed
version, and why, are documented in docs/deviations.md.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Bundled third-party code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vendor/yates/
    The Yates butterfly kernel, a verbatim copy of the `yates` package from
    "exact-yates-metal", at commit
    2f0823d585a5eb0e4b71ed368af016defa7b8d36 (recorded in vendor/KERNEL_COMMIT).
    MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Daniil Pimanov.
    Full licence text: vendor/LICENSE.exact-yates-metal
    Provenance and the reason it is vendored rather than a submodule:
    vendor/PROVENANCE.md

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Not bundled; resolved at install time and pinned in pyproject.toml / uv.lock.
Licences below are as declared by the installed package metadata.

    mlx     == 0.32.2    MIT
    numpy   == 2.5.3     BSD-3-Clause AND 0BSD AND MIT AND Zlib AND CC0-1.0
    pytest  == 9.1.1     MIT  (development only)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Referenced, but not used as code
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

neelnanda-io/Grokking -- https://github.com/neelnanda-io/Grokking
    apps/grokking reproduces the *task* posed by that repository, learning
    (a + b) mod 113, and compares against the mechanistic account of the
    grokked solution as a character algorithm. No code from it is used, and
    the model here is the character network of the paper above rather than a
    transformer.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Mathematical sources the implementation relies on
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These are published results, not licensed works. They are listed because the
code depends on them for its correctness, not only for context.

F. Yates, "The Design and Analysis of Factorial Experiments",
    Imperial Bureau of Soil Science, Technical Communication No. 35, 1937.
    The butterfly algorithm the Metal kernel implements.

A.-M. Legendre, on the p-adic valuation of factorials (Legendre's formula).
    Fixes the truncation order of the p-adic exponential in
    padic/character.py, and is what shows the printed bound in Algorithm 4 of
    the paper above loses digits (docs/deviations.md).

K. Mahler, "An interpolation series for continuous functions of a p-adic
    variable", Journal fuer die reine und angewandte Mathematik, 1958.
    The Mahler/binomial series behind Algorithm 3.

Z. I. Borevich and I. R. Shafarevich, "Number Theory", Academic Press, 1966.
    Conjectured the rationality of the Poincare series of a polynomial
    congruence.

J.-I. Igusa, on the rationality of that series, via resolution of
    singularities. This is what licenses apps/zeta to recover the whole
    generating function from finitely many exact coefficients; without it, the
    fitted rational function would be an extrapolation rather than the answer.

